Usage: agent-deck web [options]

Start the TUI with web UI server running alongside.

Options:
  -insecure-bind
    	Allow binding a non-loopback address with no --token or --token-file (UNSAFE: exposes an unauthenticated RCE surface to the network)
  -listen string
    	Listen address for web server (default "<VERSION>.1:8420")
  -no-tui
    	Run in headless mode (HTTP server only, no bubbletea TUI)
  -push
    	Enable web push notifications (auto-generates VAPID keys per profile)
  -push-test-every duration
    	Send periodic push test notifications at this interval (e.g. 10s, 1m); 0 disables
  -push-vapid-subject string
    	VAPID subject used for web push notifications (default "mailto:agentdeck@localhost")
  -read-only
    	Run in read-only mode (input disabled)
  -token string
    	Bearer token for API/WS access
  -token-file string
    	Read bearer token for API/WS access from a 0600 file (keeps the secret out of the process argv)
Examples:
  agent-deck web
  agent-deck -p work web --listen <VERSION>.1:9000
  agent-deck web --read-only
  agent-deck web --push
  agent-deck web --push --push-test-every 10s
  agent-deck web --no-tui                 # headless, perf win
  agent-deck web --no-tui --listen <VERSION>.1:9000
  agent-deck web --listen <VERSION>.0:8420 --token secret  # expose to LAN (token REQUIRED)
  agent-deck web --listen <VERSION>.0:8420 --token-file ~/.config/agent-deck/web-token

Security: the server binds loopback (<VERSION>.1) by default. Binding a
non-loopback address without --token or --token-file is refused — it would
expose an unauthenticated remote-code-execution surface. Override with
--insecure-bind (unsafe) only when you understand the risk.
--token-file must be a regular file that is not group- or world-accessible
(chmod 600); it keeps the secret out of argv, where any local user can read
it from /proc. MCP administration over HTTP is only wired when a token is
configured — an unauthenticated server keeps those routes unavailable.
